---

<PageLayout
  title="From GLP-1 to Melanocortin: How Incretins and MC4R Connect"
  seoTitle="GLP-1 and Melanocortin: How Incretins Connect to MC4R"
  description="GLP-1 receptor agonists act upstream of or parallel to the hypothalamic melanocortin circuit. Here is how leptin, POMC, α-MSH, and MC4R fit into modern metabolic pharmacology."
  kicker="Metabolic Circuitry"
  updated="2026"
  reviewed={true}
  about={[
    { name: 'Melanocortin 4 receptor', type: 'Protein' },
    { name: 'GLP-1 receptor', type: 'Protein' },
    { name: 'Pro-opiomelanocortin', type: 'Protein' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The GLP-1 era brought hypothalamic appetite biology into mainstream
    medicine. For readers coming from incretin pharmacology, the melanocortin
    system is the next node in the circuit: it is the downstream pathway through
    which long-term energy signals—including leptin and, in part, GLP-1—are
    translated into satiety and energy expenditure.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <h2>Where the two pathways meet</h2>
  <figure class="my-6 rounded-lg border border-[var(--border)] bg-[var(--bg-elevated)] p-5">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 680 150" class="w-full h-auto" role="img" aria-label="GLP-1 and leptin converge on arcuate POMC and AgRP neurons upstream of MC4R">
      <rect x="16" y="20" width="150" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      <text x="91" y="46" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="600">GLP-1 / Leptin</text>
      <rect x="16" y="86" width="150" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      <text x="91" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="13">Fasting / Ghrelin</text>

      <line x1="166" y1="42" x2="236" y2="42" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" />
      <polygon points="236,37 246,42 236,47" fill="var(--accent)" />
      <line x1="166" y1="108" x2="236" y2="108" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
      <polygon points="236,103 246,108 236,113" fill="var(--text-muted)" />

      <rect x="248" y="20" width="175" height="44" rx="8" fill="var(--accent-soft)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      <text x="335" y="46" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text)" font-size="13" font-weight="600">POMC → α-MSH (+)</text>
      <rect x="248" y="86" width="175" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5" />
      <text x="335" y="112" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="13">AgRP / NPY (−)</text>

      <line x1="423" y1="42" x2="494" y2="66" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" />
      <line x1="423" y1="108" x2="494" y2="84" stroke="var(--text-muted)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />

      <rect x="496" y="48" width="168" height="54" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" />
      <text x="580" y="72" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--accent)" font-size="14" font-weight="700">MC4R (PVN)</text>
      <text x="580" y="90" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--text-muted)" font-size="11">Satiety &amp; energy balance</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-[var(--text-muted)]">
      GLP-1 and leptin act upstream on arcuate POMC and AgRP neurons; MC4R integrates both signals in the paraventricular nucleus.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    In the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, two opposing neuronal
    populations regulate feeding: <strong>POMC/CART neurons</strong>, which
    suppress appetite, and <strong>AgRP/NPY neurons</strong>, which drive
    hunger.<Cite n={2} />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>POMC neurons</strong> cleave{' '}
      <a href="/pomc">pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC)</a> into{' '}
      <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a>, which activates{' '}
      <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> on downstream paraventricular nucleus
      (PVN) neurons to promote satiety.<Cite n={2} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AgRP neurons</strong> release <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a>, an
      endogenous antagonist and inverse agonist at MC4R that blocks α-MSH
      signaling.<Cite n={4} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GLP-1 receptor agonists</strong> (such as liraglutide and
      semaglutide) access circumventricular organs and the arcuate nucleus,
      where they directly stimulate POMC/CART neurons and indirectly inhibit
      AgRP/NPY neurons via GABAergic interneurons.<Cite n={1} />
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why MC4R sits downstream of leptin resistance</h2>
  <p>
    In common obesity, circulating leptin is high, but hypothalamic leptin
    signaling is blunted. Because leptin acts upstream of POMC transcription,
    upstream leptin resistance leaves POMC/MC4R tone under-activated. In
    monogenic obesity caused by <em>POMC</em>, <em>PCSK1</em>, or{' '}
    <em>LEPR</em> deficiency, the upstream signal is broken altogether.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>
  <p>
    Direct <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> agonists such as{' '}
    <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a> (and investigational oral
    candidates such as <a href="/bivamelagon">bivamelagon</a>) bypass the
    upstream leptin–POMC step and activate MC4R directly.<Cite n={3} />
  </p>

  <h2>Key differences between GLP-1 and MC4R pharmacology</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Feature</th>
        <th>GLP-1 Receptor Agonists</th>
        <th>MC4R Agonists</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Primary target</td>
        <td>GLP-1R (pancreas, brainstem, hypothalamus)</td>
        <td>MC4R (hypothalamic PVN, brainstem)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Relationship to POMC</td>
        <td>Upstream/parallel modulator of POMC &amp; AgRP neurons</td>
        <td>Direct downstream effector</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Approved indications</td>
        <td>Type 2 diabetes, general obesity, cardiovascular risk</td>
        <td>Monogenic &amp; syndromic obesity (POMC, LEPR, BBS)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Characteristic class effects</td>
        <td>GI motility slowing, nausea</td>
        <td>Nausea, skin pigmentation (via MC1R cross-reactivity)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Where the field is heading</h2>
  <p>
    Current clinical research is testing whether oral small-molecule MC4R
    agonists can achieve cleaner receptor selectivity—avoiding MC1R-driven
    pigmentation and blood-pressure elevations—and whether combining incretin
    therapies with direct melanocortin modulation offers additive weight loss
    in polygenic or hypothalamic obesity.
  </p>

  <References items={references} />
</PageLayout>
